CAT = "ukfoodHygiene"; SEG = "HYG"
Q = [
("You come back to the kitchen after a break. What must you do before handling food?",
 "Wash your hands", ["Change your apron", "Check the rota", "Wipe the worktop"],
 "Hands pick up bacteria during a break, so wash them before touching food or equipment. A clean apron does not make dirty hands safe."),
("When must a food handler wash their hands?",
 "After handling raw meat", ["Only at the start of a shift", "Only when they look dirty", "Only after using the toilet"],
 "Wash hands after raw food, the toilet, breaks, bins, cleaning, touching your face and before starting work. Hands can carry bacteria without looking dirty."),
("Where should food handlers wash their hands?",
 "In a hand-wash-only basin", ["In the food preparation sink", "In the pot-wash sink", "In a bowl of hot soapy water"],
 "Hand washing needs a dedicated basin with hot and cold water, soap and a hygienic way to dry hands. Washing hands in a food sink can contaminate food."),
("How should hands be dried after washing in a kitchen?",
 "With disposable paper towels", ["On a clean tea towel", "On the front of your apron", "By shaking them dry in the air"],
 "Disposable paper towels remove moisture and bacteria and are thrown away. Tea towels and aprons spread bacteria back onto clean hands."),
("When washing your hands, what comes straight after wetting them?",
 "Applying soap", ["Drying them", "Rinsing them", "Turning off the tap"],
 "Wet hands, apply soap, rub all surfaces including thumbs, fingertips and between fingers, rinse, then dry with a paper towel."),
("Which item of jewellery do kitchen hygiene policies most often allow?",
 "A plain wedding band", ["A ring set with stones", "A plain wristwatch", "Hooped earrings"],
 "Many businesses allow only a plain band, because stones, watches and earrings trap bacteria and can fall into food. Check your employer's policy."),
("Why should food handlers not wear nail varnish or false nails?",
 "They can chip into food", ["They stain chopping boards", "They slow down the service", "They react with detergent"],
 "Nail varnish can flake and false nails can fall into food as physical hazards, and both trap dirt. Keep nails short, clean and unvarnished."),
("What sort of dressing should cover a cut on a food handler's hand?",
 "A blue waterproof plaster", ["A skin-coloured plaster", "A white fabric plaster", "A clear waterproof plaster"],
 "Blue waterproof dressings are easy to see if they fall into food, and many contain a metal strip for detectors. The cut must be fully covered."),
("A food handler has diarrhoea. What should they do?",
 "Report it and stay off work", ["Work but wash hands more often", "Work only on dry goods", "Work wearing gloves"],
 "Food handlers with diarrhoea or vomiting must report it to their manager and stay away from food handling, because they can easily pass infection on."),
("After diarrhoea or vomiting stops, how long should a food handler usually wait before returning to work?",
 "48 hours", ["12 hours", "24 hours", "7 days"],
 "FSA guidance is to stay away until 48 hours after symptoms have stopped, without medicine to stop them. Some infections need longer, on medical advice."),
("Which of these should a food handler report to their manager before starting work?",
 "An infected cut on a finger", ["A sprained left ankle from sport", "A pulled back muscle", "A new phone number"],
 "Infected cuts and skin infections can carry Staphylococcus aureus onto food, so they must be reported. The manager decides what work, if any, is safe."),
("Why must food handlers not smoke in food rooms?",
 "Ash and germs can reach food", ["It warms up the nearby fridges", "It sets off the chemicals", "Smoke dries the food out"],
 "Smoking is banned in enclosed workplaces anyway, and in food rooms it adds the risk of ash and of bacteria passing from mouth to fingers to food."),
("Why must food handlers cover or tie back their hair?",
 "To stop hair falling into food", ["To keep the hair from grease", "To show who works in the kitchen", "To keep the head cool"],
 "Hair is a common physical contaminant and can carry bacteria. A hat or net and tied-back hair keep it out of food."),
("What is the main purpose of protective clothing in a kitchen?",
 "To keep contamination off food", ["To keep staff clothes smart", "To show each person's job rank", "To keep staff warm at work"],
 "Clean protective clothing stops dirt, hair and bacteria from everyday clothes reaching food. It should be light-coloured, clean and worn only at work."),
("When should you put on your kitchen whites?",
 "On arrival at work", ["At home before you travel", "Once food prep has started", "Only for an inspection"],
 "Change into clean protective clothing at work, so it is not contaminated on the journey. Put it on before you start handling food."),
("Before stepping outside for a break, what should you do with your apron?",
 "Take it off", ["Tuck it in", "Turn it round", "Keep it on"],
 "Remove aprons and overalls before going outside, to the toilet or on a break, so they do not pick up contamination and bring it back to food."),
("When using disposable gloves, what must you still do?",
 "Wash hands before wearing them", ["Wash the gloves between tasks", "Keep one pair on for the whole shift", "Reuse them after a break"],
 "Gloves are only as clean as the hands that put them on. Wash hands first and change gloves between tasks, especially after raw food."),
("Why must you never sneeze or cough over food?",
 "It spreads bacteria onto food", ["It cools the food too fast", "It changes the food's colour", "It lowers the hygiene rating score"],
 "The nose and mouth carry bacteria such as Staphylococcus aureus. Turn away, use a tissue, then wash your hands."),
("How should a chef taste food during cooking?",
 "With a clean spoon each time", ["With a clean finger, then wash", "With the stirring spoon", "Straight from the ladle"],
 "Use a clean spoon for each taste and do not put it back in the food. Fingers and shared spoons carry mouth bacteria into the pot."),
("Where may kitchen staff eat their meals?",
 "In a staff area away from food", ["At the prep bench when it is quiet", "In the walk-in chiller", "At the pass between orders"],
 "Eating in food rooms risks contamination from hands and mouths and leaves food debris. Staff should eat in a separate area."),
("Why is touching your nose while preparing food a hygiene risk?",
 "Noses can carry Staph aureus", ["It spreads norovirus to the nose", "It contaminates the air", "It causes allergic reactions"],
 "Many healthy people carry Staphylococcus aureus in their noses. If you touch your face, wash your hands before going back to food."),
("Which condition should a food handler report before starting work?",
 "Jaundice (yellow skin)", ["A twisted ankle at home", "Tired eyes after a late night", "A short haircut"],
 "FSA guidance lists jaundice with diarrhoea, vomiting, stomach pain, nausea, fever and infected skin, nose or throat as symptoms to report. The manager decides what work, if any, is safe."),
("Why should food handlers keep their fingernails short?",
 "Dirt collects under long nails", ["Long nails tear disposable gloves", "Long nails slow down prep", "Short nails dry quicker"],
 "Dirt and bacteria collect under long nails and are hard to wash out. Short, clean nails are part of good personal hygiene."),
("Which type of tap is best at a hand-wash basin?",
 "Knee- or sensor-operated", ["A standard twist-top tap", "A cold water only tap", "A garden-style hose"],
 "Taps that do not need to be touched by hand stop clean hands picking up bacteria from the tap. Hot and cold water are needed."),
("What should a kitchen hand-wash basin have?",
 "Hot and cold water and soap", ["Cold running water only", "A plug for soaking utensils", "A food waste disposal"],
 "Hand-wash basins need hot and cold (or mixed) running water, soap and hygienic drying. They are kept for hand washing only."),
("Why should food handlers not wear strong perfume or aftershave?",
 "It can taint the food", ["It kills bacteria on food", "It melts in the kitchen heat", "It triggers smoke alarms"],
 "Strong scents can be absorbed by food, especially fats and dairy, and change its taste. Keep scents light or skip them at work."),
("A colleague vomited last night but feels fine now. What should happen?",
 "They stay off for 48 hours", ["They work but avoid raw meat", "They work wearing gloves", "They take a tablet and work"],
 "Feeling better does not mean they are no longer infectious. Food handlers stay away until 48 hours after symptoms stop."),
("Why is hand sanitiser gel not a replacement for hand washing?",
 "It does not remove dirt", ["It is too expensive", "It contains food allergens", "It dries too slowly"],
 "Gel does not remove dirt or grease and does not work well against every germ, such as norovirus. It can be used after washing, not instead of it."),
("Why should mobile phones not be used while preparing food?",
 "They carry bacteria to hands", ["They overheat near ovens", "They upset waiting customers", "They affect the probe"],
 "Phones are touched often and rarely cleaned, so they carry bacteria. If you must use one, wash your hands before going back to food."),
("How should ready-to-eat food be handled?",
 "With clean tongs or gloves", ["With bare hands", "After wiping hands on an apron", "After rinsing hands in the sink"],
 "Ready-to-eat food will not be cooked again, so handle it as little as possible and use clean utensils or gloves."),
("In food hygiene, what is a carrier?",
 "A healthy person carrying pathogens", ["A person who delivers the food", "A worker who has a known food allergy", "An insect that spreads germs"],
 "A carrier has harmful bacteria in their body, often in the gut, without symptoms. Good hand washing protects food even from staff who feel well."),
("When should disposable gloves be changed?",
 "When you change task", ["Only at the end of a shift", "Only if they tear or split", "Every two hours of work"],
 "Change gloves between tasks, especially between raw and ready-to-eat food, and whenever they are torn or dirty."),
]
